<script>
(function () {
  "use strict";
  /* ---------- clue data (Lessons 51–54) ---------- */
  const IMG_LIG = "data:image/jpeg;base64,";
const L51="Lesson 51: Structure and Function of the Female Reproductive System";
const L52="Lesson 52: Structure of the Male Reproductive System";
const L53="Lesson 53: Function of the Female Reproductive System";
const L53M="Lesson 53: Function of the Male Reproductive System section";
const L54="Lesson 54: Drugs in LA Repro and Surgical Drugs";
const LV={100:1,200:2,300:3,400:3,500:4};

const CATS=[
{name:"Female Anatomy",lec:"L51",clues:[
 {v:100,type:"Lesion localization",topic:"Right vs left ovary position in the bitch",
  stem:"A 1-year-old bitch is undergoing ovariohysterectomy, and the surgeon has found the left ovary easily. Where should the surgeon expect to find the right ovary relative to the left?",
  ch:["More caudal","More cranial","More ventral","At the pelvic inlet","Lateral to the colon"],a:1,
  why:"In dogs and cats the right ovary lies more cranially than the left, which makes it harder to reach during a spay. It sits dorsal to the descending duodenum and ascending colon.",
  wr:["Opposite of the true relationship. This would be correct if asked where the left ovary lies relative to the right.",null,"Both ovaries lie ventral to the lumbar vertebrae, high in the dorsal abdomen. The defining difference between them is cranial versus caudal, not ventral position.","The cow's ovaries make the most caudal migration and sit just cranial to the pelvic inlet. Correct for a cow, not a bitch.","The left ovary lies between the descending colon and the abdominal wall. Correct if the question asked about the left ovary."],
  src:L51+", slide 9"},
 {v:200,type:"Next best step",topic:"Suspensory ligament during spay",img:true,
  stem:"A 2-year-old deep-chested bitch is undergoing ovariohysterectomy, and the left ovary cannot be brought up to the incision. Which labeled structure should the surgeon break down to exteriorize the ovary?",
  ch:["Structure A","Structure B","Structure C","Structure D","Structure E"],a:2,
  why:"C is the suspensory ligament, the cranial free edge of the mesovarium anchoring the ovary to the last few ribs and transversalis fascia. It is ruptured or strummed during a spay to free the ovary for removal.",
  wr:["A is the mesosalpinx, which holds the uterine tube and helps form the ovarian bursa. Correct if asked which fold joins the mesovarium to form the bursa.","B is the proper ligament of the ovary, joining the ovary to the uterine horn. Correct if asked which ligament is the caudal continuation of the mesovarium's free edge.",null,"D is the round ligament, which passes through the inguinal canal in the bitch. Correct if asked about a structure that predisposes to inguinal herniation of the uterus.","E is the mesometrium, which carries the uterine vessels. Correct if asked which part of the broad ligament conducts the uterine vessels."],
  src:L51+", slides 26–27; "+L54+", slide 75"},
 {v:300,type:"Next best step",topic:"Passing the AI gun through the bovine cervix",
  stem:"During artificial insemination of a 4-year-old Holstein cow, the catheter passes easily through the vagina and then stops in a blind recess that rings the cervix. What is the most appropriate next step?",
  ch:["Deposit semen at that site","Switch to a spirette catheter","Redirect at a craniodorsal angle","Guide the cervix per rectum","Pass a vaginal speculum first"],a:3,
  why:"In the cow the vaginal fornix forms a ring around the portio vaginalis and is the classic point of obstruction. The firm cervix is the landmark palpated per rectum, and the hand twists and bends it onto the gun until the rings slide over the tip.",
  wr:["Semen must be deposited just past the end of the cervix, not in the fornix.","The spirette is for the sow, whose cervix has no portio vaginalis or fornix and whose interlocking pulvini block a regular catheter.","The craniodorsal insertion angle is for the bitch, because of the caudoventral inclination of her tract.",null,"A speculum is used in the mare to judge whether the cervix looks estrous. It does not get a catheter past the bovine fornix."],
  src:L51+", slides 6, 42, 53, 63, 67"},
 {v:400,type:"Management",topic:"Windsucking and Caslick's procedure",
  stem:"A 6-year-old Thoroughbred mare has recurrent endometritis and infertility. Her anus is sunken cranially, the dorsal vulvar commissure sits above the pelvic floor, and air is heard entering the vagina as she walks. Which procedure best corrects the underlying problem?",
  ch:["Incision of the craniodorsal vagina","Suturing of the dorsal vulva","Swabbing of the clitoral sinus","Ligation of the uterine artery","Breakdown of the suspensory ligament"],a:1,
  why:"Elevated dorsal commissure → sunken anus, raised vestibular floor and an inefficient constrictor vulvae → windsucking and fecal contamination → endometritis. Caslick's procedure sutures the upper vulva to restore the seal.",
  wr:["The craniodorsal vaginal wall gives surgical access to the peritoneal cavity, used for ovarian surgery in horses. Correct if asked for an approach to the ovary.",null,"The clitoral sinus can harbor bacteria such as those of contagious equine metritis. Swabbing would fit a screening question, but it does not fix conformation.","The uterine artery is palpated per rectum for fremitus in pregnancy. Ligating uterine vessels is a spay step in small animals.","Rupturing the suspensory ligament frees the ovary during a canine spay. It has no role in perineal conformation."],
  src:L51+", slides 65, 69, 71–72"},
 {v:500,type:"Next best step",topic:"Fremitus before prostaglandin treatment",
  stem:"A 5-year-old Holstein cow with no breeding records is scheduled for dinoprost as part of a herd synchronization program. On rectal palpation, the artery coursing in the mesometrium has a strong, buzzing pulsation. What is the most appropriate next step?",
  ch:["Give dinoprost as scheduled","Give gonadorelin, then dinoprost","Insert a progesterone device","Give dexamethasone instead","Withhold prostaglandin treatment"],a:4,
  why:"The vessel in the mesometrium is the uterine artery, and a palpable thrill (fremitus) from the third month onward is diagnostic of pregnancy. Prostaglandin F2α causes luteolysis and is used to terminate pregnancy, so giving it would abort her.",
  wr:["Correct only for a confirmed open cow. Here dinoprost would lyse the CL and end the pregnancy.","The GnRH–PGF2α–GnRH protocol still contains a luteolytic dose. Correct for an open cow entering fixed-time AI.","Progestins are used for synchronization and to maintain pregnancy, but a pregnant cow does not need synchronizing. Correct for an open cow on a progestin protocol.","Dexamethasone induces parturition in cattle near term. Correct only if she were close to term and induction were wanted.",null],
  src:L51+", slides 45–46, 69; "+L54+", slides 15, 24, 27"}
]},
{name:"Male Anatomy",lec:"L52",clues:[
 {v:100,type:"Management",topic:"Castration approach in the tom",
  stem:"A 7-month-old domestic shorthair tom is presented for routine castration. Given where his scrotum sits, which castration method is typically used?",
  ch:["Scrotal incision","Prescrotal incision","Inguinal incision","Ventral midline incision","Burdizzo clamping"],a:0,
  why:"The cat's scrotum is sub-anal in the perineum and held tightly against it, so castration is done through a scrotal incision.",
  wr:[null,"The prescrotal approach is common in dogs, whose scrotum is pendulous and inguinal.","An inguinal approach fits a testis retained in the inguinal canal, as in cryptorchidism.","A midline abdominal approach is used for ovariohysterectomy and for an abdominally retained testis.","The Burdizzo is applied to the long scrotal neck of ruminants."],
  src:L52+", slides 6, 26, 71, 73; "+L54+", slides 51, 55"},
 {v:200,type:"Lesion localization",topic:"Bovine epididymal tail position",
  stem:"During a breeding soundness exam on a 3-year-old Hereford bull, the clinician wants to evaluate the largest and most easily palpated part of the epididymis. Where relative to the testis should she palpate?",
  ch:["Dorsal pole","Cranial pole","Ventral pole","Caudodorsal aspect","Lateral surface"],a:2,
  why:"In the bull the epididymis lies medial to the vertically oriented testis, with the head covering the dorsal third and the large, prominent tail at the ventral pole. The tail is what you palpate in a BSE.",
